
3.1 SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE
XYZ implements a tool-calling agent architecture that combines large language model reasoning
with specialized time series analysis tools through a human-in-the-loop workflow (Figure [STUB:
architecture diagram]). The system leverages native LLM tool-calling for flexible reasoning and
dynamic tool selection conditioned on task context. It operates in two phases: (1) Planning, where
dataset profiling and LLM-based query understanding generate a structured UserTaskPlan pre-
sented for user approval, and (2) Execution, where the LLM agent iteratively selects and invokes
tools based on the approved plan, using intermediate outputs to guide subsequent decisions.
Phase 1: Planning and Human Approval
3.1.1 DATA PROFILING.
An automated profiler analyzes temporal properties (frequency inference, date range validation),
statistical characteristics (mean, standard deviation, min, max, missing value percentage), station-
arity (Augmented Dickey–Fuller and KPSS tests), seasonality (autocorrelation analysis and STL
decomposition), and outliers (IQR-based flagging). The profiler produces a DataProfile object
containing summary statistics only; raw data are never exposed to the LLM, ensuring scalability and
preventing data hallucination.
3.1.2 QUERY UNDERSTANDING.
Users submit natural language queries (e.g., forecasting future consumption or detecting anoma-
lies). A low-temperature LLM (temperature=0.1) extracts structured task specifications including
task type (forecast, anomaly, or both), forecast horizon (in time steps), univariate or multivariate
mode, selected feature columns, cross-validation requirements, and explainability/reporting needs.
The LLM receives only data profile statistics (e.g., frequency, seasonality period, trend indicators,
stationarity p-values) as context. The resulting plan is validated using a Pydantic UserTaskPlan
schema and presented for user approval via Streamlit UI or CLI
3.1.3 HUMAN APPROVAL GATE.
Users may modify task configuration (type, horizon, multivariate settings), override model selection,
enable or disable cross-validation, explainability, and reporting, and adjust feature columns. This ap-
proval mechanism preserves domain expert control while mitigating reliability concerns associated
with fully autonomous LLM agents in critical forecasting applications.
Phase 2: Tool-Calling Execution
After approval, the agent enters an iterative tool-calling loop (maximum 25 steps).
3.1.4 MODEL TRAINING TOOLS.
Model selection depends on task characteristics. Univariate models include Naive, Seasonal Naive,
ARIMA (via pmdarima auto arima), ETS, N-BEATS, and TinyTimeMixer. Random For-
est and LightGBM support both univariate (autoregressive) and multivariate (exogenous feature)
modes. Models are trained using train forecast model(model name, horizon) with
rolling-origin cross-validation (minimum training size max(50, 2× horizon), adaptive step size),
grid-search hyperparameter tuning, and validation metrics (MAPE, RMSE, MAE) reported as mean
and standard deviation across folds. The agent receives structured validation outputs and may itera-
tively refine model selection.
